Relational strictification of an approximable map keeps the original relation and requires that a master input be related only to the master output. Domain and codomain membership are those of the original relation. The masters are related, and the extra clause holds by reflexivity on the master output. An intersection on the right intersects the original relations; on a master input both outputs equal the master, so their intersection does as well. Monotonicity keeps the original monotonicity. If the smaller input equals the master, the larger input equals the master by antisymmetry with its inclusion in the master, the smaller output is then the master, and the larger output equals the master by antisymmetry with its inclusion in the master.
